/* Generated by tools/build.js from data/cisa.js. Edit that file, not this one. */
CertHub.addQuestions("cisa", [["ca1",0,1,"While planning the annual IS audit plan, what should the audit manager do FIRST?",["Schedule audits of every system in rotation","Ask IT management which areas they want audited","Select the systems with the most recent incidents","Perform a risk assessment of the audit universe"],3,"A risk-based plan starts by assessing risk across the audit universe so limited resources go to the areas that matter most. Asking IT which areas to audit sounds collaborative but hands scope to the auditee and weakens independence.","Outline 1A3",["A fixed rotation ignores risk, so high-risk areas may wait years while low-risk ones are audited needlessly.","Management input is useful, but letting the auditee choose the scope threatens independence and may steer audits away from weak areas.","Recent incidents are one risk signal, but choosing only on incidents skips a structured assessment of the whole audit universe.",null],null],["ca2",0,1,"An IS auditor tests whether each sampled change request was approved before it was moved to production. What kind of test is this?",["A substantive test of transactions","A walkthrough of the process design","A test of details of account balances","A compliance test of a control"],3,"Checking that an approval control operated for sampled changes is a compliance (control) test. Substantive tests look at the data or balances themselves for errors, not at whether a control operated.","Outline 1B2",["Substantive tests examine transactions or balances for misstatement; here the auditor is checking whether a control operated.","A walkthrough traces one transaction to understand the design; testing a sample for approval evidence tests operating effectiveness.","Tests of balances are substantive financial-audit procedures and do not test whether the approval control worked.",null],null],["ca3",0,1,"An auditor finds that a control previously tested as effective failed in three of the 40 items sampled this year. What is the MOST appropriate next step?",["Evaluate the deviations and expand substantive testing","Report the control as effective because most items passed","Stop the audit and escalate the matter to the board","Replace the three failed items with new sample items"],0,"Deviations above the tolerable rate mean the control may not be reliable, so the auditor evaluates the causes and increases substantive work. Swapping out failed items would hide the result, which is never acceptable.","Outline 1B2",[null,"A 7.5% deviation rate may exceed the tolerable rate, so calling the control effective without evaluation is unsupported.","Three deviations call for evaluation and more testing, not an immediate board escalation that skips the analysis.","Replacing failed sample items with new ones hides exceptions and invalidates the sample."],null],["ca4",0,1,"Which type of evidence is generally the MOST reliable?",["A written statement prepared by the process owner","Oral answers given by the system administrator","Evidence the auditor obtains by reperformance","A screenshot supplied by the auditee by email"],2,"Evidence the auditor produces personally, such as reperforming a control or calculation, is more reliable than evidence prepared or supplied by the auditee. Written statements from management are still auditee-generated.","Outline 1B3",["A statement written by the process owner comes from the auditee and has not been independently verified.","Oral evidence from inquiry is the weakest form and must be corroborated.",null,"A screenshot provided by the auditee could be selective or altered; the auditor did not obtain it directly."],null],["ca5",0,1,"An IS auditor was the project lead who designed a new payroll interface last year. The auditor is now assigned to audit that interface. What is the BEST course of action?",["Disclose the impairment and request reassignment","Perform the audit, since the auditor knows the system best","Perform the audit but exclude the interface design from scope","Ask the payroll manager to approve the assignment"],0,"Auditing one's own work is a self-review threat to independence. The auditor should disclose it to audit management, who should reassign the work. Narrowing the scope does not remove the impairment.","Outline 1A1",[null,"Deep familiarity is exactly the problem: auditing your own design is a self-review threat.","Excluding the design from scope still leaves the auditor reviewing a system they built, and it weakens the audit.","The auditee cannot waive an independence impairment; audit management decides."],null],["ca6",0,1,"Which sampling method is BEST when the auditor wants to estimate the total dollar value of errors in a large population of invoices?",["Attribute sampling","Monetary unit (dollar unit) sampling","Discovery sampling","Stop-or-go sampling"],1,"Monetary unit sampling (a form of variable sampling) selects items in proportion to value and estimates monetary misstatement. Attribute sampling estimates a rate of occurrence, such as how often a control fails, not a dollar amount.","Outline 1B2",["Attribute sampling estimates how often something occurs (a rate), not the monetary size of errors.",null,"Discovery sampling is used to find at least one example of a rare event such as fraud, not to estimate total value.","Stop-or-go sampling limits sample size when few errors are expected; it estimates a rate, not a dollar total."],null],["ca7",0,1,"During fieldwork, an auditor suspects that a database administrator is altering vendor payment records. What should the auditor do FIRST?",["Confront the administrator to get an explanation","Expand testing to prove the fraud fully before telling anyone","Report the suspicion to audit management and the appropriate authority","Disable the administrator's database account"],2,"Auditors are not investigators and must not tip off the suspect. The right move is to communicate the indicators to audit management and the appropriate level of the organization, who decide on an investigation. Disabling accounts is a management action.","Outline 1A1",["Confronting the suspect can tip them off, destroy evidence and compromise any later investigation.","Proving fraud is the job of a trained investigation; the auditor's duty is to report indicators promptly.",null,"Disabling accounts is an operational decision for management, and doing it would impair the auditor's independence."],null],["ca8",0,1,"Which control type is a reconciliation of daily batch totals between two systems?",["Preventive","Deterrent","Detective","Directive"],2,"A reconciliation finds errors after they have occurred, so it is a detective control. A preventive control, such as an input edit check, stops the error before it gets in.","Outline 1A4",["Preventive controls stop errors before they happen; a reconciliation compares results after processing.","Deterrent controls discourage behavior, such as warning banners; they do not compare totals.",null,"Directive controls tell people what to do, such as policies; they do not find errors."],null],["ca9",0,1,"An auditor wants to test every transaction in a year of purchasing data for split purchases just under the approval limit. Which approach is MOST effective?",["Select a random sample of 60 purchase orders","Interview purchasing staff about split purchases","Use data analytics to test the full population","Review the purchasing policy for approval limits"],2,"Data analytics tools (CAATs) can test 100% of transactions for patterns such as multiple orders to the same vendor just under a limit. A sample would likely miss a pattern that spans several transactions.","Outline 1B4",["A random sample of individual orders is unlikely to catch split purchases, which only show up across related orders.","Interviews rely on the people who might be splitting purchases and give weak evidence.",null,"Reviewing the policy shows the rule exists but does not test whether it was circumvented."],null],["ca10",0,1,"What is the PRIMARY purpose of an audit charter?",["To set out the audit mandate and authority","To list the audits planned for the coming year","To describe the detailed test steps for each engagement","To record management's responses to audit findings"],0,"The charter, approved by the board or audit committee, gives the audit function its mandate, authority, independence and access. The annual plan lists planned audits and audit programs hold test steps.","Outline 1A1",[null,"The list of planned audits is the annual audit plan, which changes each year; the charter is a lasting mandate.","Detailed test steps belong in an audit program for a specific engagement.","Management responses are recorded in audit reports and follow-up tracking, not in the charter."],null],["ca11",0,1,"Management has agreed to fix a high-risk finding within 90 days. After 90 days, what should the IS auditor do?",["Close the finding, since management formally committed to fixing it","Implement the fix to make sure it is done correctly","Reissue the original report with the same finding","Perform follow-up to verify the corrective action was implemented"],3,"Follow-up confirms that agreed actions were actually taken and that they address the risk. Auditors do not implement fixes themselves, because that would impair their independence.","Outline 1B5",["A promise is not evidence; closing without verification leaves the risk possibly unaddressed.","Implementing the fix would make the auditor responsible for the control and impair independence.","Reissuing the report does not show whether anything changed; follow-up testing is needed first.",null],null],["ca12",0,1,"Which risk is the auditor able to reduce directly by increasing sample size and improving audit procedures?",["Detection risk","Inherent risk","Control risk","Business risk"],0,"Audit risk combines inherent, control and detection risk. Only detection risk, the chance the auditor's procedures miss a material error, is under the auditor's control. Inherent and control risk belong to the auditee's environment.","Outline 1A3",[null,"Inherent risk exists before any controls and is a property of the process, not something audit procedures change.","Control risk depends on how well the auditee's controls work; the auditor assesses it but cannot reduce it.","Business risk relates to the organization's objectives and is managed by management, not by audit procedures."],null],["ca13",0,1,"An organization wants to review transactions as they are processed and flag unusual ones immediately. Which technique fits BEST?",["An annual manual review of transaction logs","Parallel simulation run at year end","A questionnaire sent to process owners","An embedded audit module in the application"],3,"An embedded audit module (a continuous auditing technique) sits inside the application and flags transactions that meet audit criteria as they happen. Parallel simulation reprocesses data later, so it is not real time.","Outline 1B4",["An annual manual review is periodic and far too late to flag transactions as they happen.","Parallel simulation reprocesses production data with the auditor's own program after the fact, not in real time.","Questionnaires gather opinions about controls and cannot flag individual transactions.",null],null],["ca14",0,1,"Which activity is part of an audit function's quality assurance and improvement program?",["Periodic external assessment of conformance with audit standards","Approval of the internal audit budget by the chief information officer","Rotation of IT staff between system teams","Tracking of IT help desk ticket volumes"],0,"A quality assurance and improvement program includes ongoing internal monitoring and periodic external assessments of whether the audit function conforms with standards. The CIO approving the audit budget would actually threaten independence.","Outline 1B6",[null,"Budget approval by the CIO would put the audited function in control of audit resources, which harms independence.","Rotating IT staff is an IT operations control, not a quality program for the audit function.","Help desk ticket volumes measure IT service, not the quality of audit work."],null],["ca15",0,1,"An audit combines a review of financial controls with a review of the IT general controls that support the same accounting system. What type of audit is this?",["A forensic audit","A compliance audit","An integrated audit","An operational audit"],2,"An integrated audit combines financial, operational and IT audit work to give an overall assessment of controls over a process or system. A forensic audit investigates suspected fraud or crime for possible legal use.","Outline 1A2",["A forensic audit is focused on gathering evidence about suspected fraud or crime, often for legal proceedings.","A compliance audit tests adherence to specific laws, regulations or policies rather than combining disciplines.",null,"An operational audit looks at efficiency and effectiveness of operations, not a combined financial and IT review."],null],["ca16",0,1,"Which element of an audit finding describes what the auditor actually observed?",["Condition","Criteria","Cause","Effect"],0,"A finding compares the condition (what is) with the criteria (what should be), explains the cause (why the gap exists) and the effect (the risk or impact). Criteria are the standard or policy, not the observation.","Outline 1B5",[null,"Criteria are the policy, standard or expectation the condition is compared against.","Cause explains why the gap occurred, such as a missing procedure or lack of training.","Effect describes the risk or impact that results from the gap."],null],["ca17",0,2,"Who holds ultimate accountability for the governance of enterprise IT?",["The chief information officer","The IT steering committee","The chief audit executive","The board of directors"],3,"Governance, including IT governance, is the responsibility of the board, which sets direction and oversees management. The CIO manages IT within that direction, and audit provides independent assurance.","Outline 2A2",["The CIO manages IT and executes strategy, but accountability for governance rests with the board.","The steering committee prioritizes and monitors IT investments on management's behalf; it is not ultimately accountable.","The chief audit executive gives independent assurance on governance; owning it would impair independence.",null],null],["ca18",0,2,"An IS auditor reviewing IT strategy finds that IT projects are approved by the IT department alone. What is the GREATEST risk?",["IT investments may not support business objectives","Project managers may lack formal project management certification","Projects may use outdated technology","IT staff may be overworked"],0,"Without business participation (for example through a steering committee), IT may fund projects that do not align with business strategy, which is the core governance concern. The other issues are narrower operational risks.","Outline 2A2",[null,"Certification of project managers is a narrow staffing matter, not the main governance risk of IT-only approval.","Technology choice is a secondary concern; the bigger issue is whether projects deliver business value.","Workload is an operational concern that does not stem directly from who approves projects."],null],["ca19",0,2,"In a small IT department, one administrator both develops and moves code into production because there is no one else. What should the auditor recommend?",["Independent review of production changes as a compensating control","Hiring a second administrator immediately, regardless of the cost involved","Outsourcing all development to a vendor","Accepting the risk with no further action"],0,"When full segregation of duties is not practical, compensating controls such as independent review of change logs and supervisory approval reduce the risk. Hiring may not be cost-justified, and ignoring the risk is not an audit recommendation.","Outline 2B1",[null,"Adding headcount may not be cost-justified for a small team; compensating controls address the risk more practically.","Outsourcing is a large business decision that does not by itself solve the control gap.","Auditors report the risk and suggest mitigation; recommending no action ignores a real segregation-of-duties gap."],null],["ca20",0,2,"A company is outsourcing payroll processing to a cloud provider. Which contract clause is MOST important from an audit perspective?",["A clause fixing the price for five years","A clause requiring the vendor to use a specific database","A right-to-audit clause or access to SOC reports","A clause naming the vendor's account manager"],2,"Outsourcing transfers the work but not the accountability. The organization must be able to verify the vendor's controls, either by auditing them or by relying on independent reports such as SOC 2 Type 2.","Outline 2B2",["Price terms matter commercially but give no assurance about controls over sensitive payroll data.","Mandating a specific database is a technical preference that does not provide assurance on controls.",null,"Naming a contact is administrative and does nothing to verify the vendor's controls."],null],["ca21",0,2,"An auditor relies on a service provider's SOC 2 Type 1 report. What is the main limitation of this report?",["It covers only financial reporting controls","It does not test whether controls operated effectively over time","It is prepared and signed by the service provider's own management","It cannot include security criteria"],1,"A Type 1 report describes control design at a point in time. A Type 2 report also tests operating effectiveness over a period, which is what an auditor usually needs to rely on the controls.","Outline 2B2",["Financial reporting controls are the focus of SOC 1; SOC 2 covers trust services criteria such as security.",null,"SOC reports are issued by an independent auditor, not by the service provider.","Security is the one criterion every SOC 2 report includes."],null],["ca22",0,2,"Who should be responsible for classifying a customer database?",["The database administrator","The data owner in the business","The information security officer","The IS auditor"],1,"The data owner, a business manager accountable for the data, decides its classification and who may access it. The DBA is a custodian who implements the protections the owner chooses.","Outline 2A7",["The DBA is a custodian who implements controls; classifying the data is the owner's decision.",null,"The security officer sets the classification scheme and advises, but the business owner classifies the data.","The auditor evaluates whether classification is done well; doing it would impair independence."],null],["ca23",0,2,"Which document should be reviewed FIRST to understand management's intent for information security?",["The firewall configuration standard","The incident response runbook","The information security policy","The security team's job descriptions"],2,"Policies are high-level statements of management intent approved by senior management. Standards, procedures and runbooks derive from policy, so the auditor starts with the policy to know what everything else should support.","Outline 2A3",["A configuration standard implements policy for one technology; it does not state overall intent.","A runbook is a detailed procedure for one process and derives from policy.",null,"Job descriptions define roles but not management's intent for security."],null],["ca24",0,2,"An organization collects customer data for order fulfillment and later wants to use it for marketing analytics. Which privacy principle is MOST at risk?",["Data accuracy","Availability","Purpose limitation","Non-repudiation"],2,"Purpose limitation says personal data collected for one purpose should not be used for a new, incompatible purpose without a legal basis such as consent. Accuracy and availability are important but are not what this reuse threatens.","Outline 2A6",["Accuracy concerns whether data is correct and current, which the new use does not directly affect.","Availability is a security objective about access to systems, not a privacy principle about use.",null,"Non-repudiation is about proving who performed an action, not about how personal data may be used."],null],["ca25",0,2,"Which measure BEST shows whether IT is delivering value to the business?",["The number of servers managed per administrator","A balanced scorecard linked to business goals","The percentage of IT budget spent on hardware","The count of help desk tickets closed"],1,"An IT balanced scorecard links IT measures to business outcomes across financial, customer, internal process and learning perspectives. Server counts and ticket counts measure activity, not value.","Outline 2B3",["Servers per administrator is an efficiency measure that says nothing about business value.",null,"Budget split shows where money goes, not what benefit the business receives.","Tickets closed measures activity; it does not show whether IT supports business goals."],null],["ca26",0,2,"What is the PRIMARY purpose of enterprise architecture?",["To document every server's hardware specification and serial number","To choose the cheapest vendor for each technology","To align technology components with business processes and strategy","To replace the need for an IT strategy"],2,"Enterprise architecture describes how business processes, information, applications and technology fit together and how they should evolve to support strategy. It works with the IT strategy, not in place of it.","Outline 2A4",["An asset inventory records hardware details; enterprise architecture is about structure and alignment.","Cost is one factor, but architecture aims at alignment and coherence, not simply the lowest price.",null,"Architecture supports and implements strategy; it does not replace it."],null],["ca27",0,2,"In an enterprise risk management program, who should decide whether to accept a residual IT risk?",["The IS auditor who identified the risk","The business owner with the appropriate authority","The network administrator","Any member of the IT department"],1,"Risk acceptance is a management decision made by someone with authority for the affected business area and within the risk appetite. Auditors identify and report risk but never accept it for management.","Outline 2A5",["Accepting risk would make the auditor part of management and impair independence.",null,"A network administrator lacks the business authority to accept risk on the organization's behalf.","Risk acceptance must come from an accountable owner with authority, not any IT staff member."],null],["ca28",0,2,"Which is the BEST reason to require mandatory vacations for staff in sensitive IT roles?",["To reduce overtime and temporary staffing costs for the department","To comply with software licensing terms","To make sure backups are tested regularly","To help detect fraud or errors that need ongoing concealment"],3,"When someone is away, another person performs their duties and may uncover irregularities the absent employee was hiding. This makes mandatory leave a detective control, much like job rotation.","Outline 2B1",["Reducing overtime is not the control purpose; the point is detecting concealed fraud or errors.","Software licensing terms have nothing to do with staff leave.","Backup testing is a separate operational control unrelated to vacations.",null],null],["ca29",0,2,"A new data protection law applies to the organization. What should the IS auditor verify FIRST?",["That the IT budget has been increased","That management has assessed the law's requirements and their impact","That all staff have signed new contracts","That strong encryption has been deployed on every system and database"],1,"Compliance starts with management identifying applicable requirements and assessing the gap and impact. Specific measures like encryption or contract changes follow from that assessment.","Outline 2A1",["A budget increase might follow the assessment but does not show the requirements are understood.",null,"New contracts may or may not be needed; that depends on the requirements assessment.","Encryption everywhere is a specific control that may not match what the law requires; assessment comes first."],null],["ca30",0,2,"Which framework is designed specifically for the governance and management of enterprise IT?",["ITIL","ISO 9001","PRINCE2","COBIT"],3,"COBIT, published by ISACA, is a governance and management framework for enterprise information and technology. ITIL focuses on IT service management practices, ISO 9001 on quality management, and PRINCE2 on project management.","Outline 2A3",["ITIL is a service management framework; it supports management but is not a governance framework.","ISO 9001 is a general quality management standard, not specific to IT governance.","PRINCE2 is a project management method, not an enterprise IT governance framework.",null],null],["ca31",0,2,"Which situation is the BIGGEST segregation-of-duties concern?",["A help desk analyst can reset user passwords after verifying identity","A developer can read the test environment logs","A security administrator can also approve their own access requests","An operator can view the batch job schedule"],2,"Being able to request and approve one's own access defeats the approval control entirely. The other options are normal duties for those roles.","Outline 2B1",["Password resets are a normal help desk task, provided identity is verified.","Developers reading test logs is expected and does not combine incompatible duties.",null,"Operators viewing the job schedule is part of their job."],null],["ca32",0,2,"An organization rates its IT processes on a scale from 0 (incomplete) to 5 (optimizing). What is this assessment MOST useful for?",["Proving compliance with data privacy law","Identifying gaps between current and target capability","Calculating the annual loss expectancy of assets","Replacing the need for audit testing"],1,"Capability or maturity assessments compare current process capability with a target level, which helps plan improvements. They do not prove legal compliance or replace testing of controls.","Outline 2B4",["Maturity ratings are self-assessments of capability and do not prove legal compliance.",null,"Annual loss expectancy is a quantitative risk calculation, unrelated to process capability ratings.","A maturity rating is not evidence that specific controls operate; audit testing is still needed."],null],["ca33",0,3,"A project has a planned value of $400,000, an earned value of $320,000 and an actual cost of $400,000. What does this show?",["Under budget and ahead of schedule","On budget but behind schedule","Over budget but ahead of schedule","Over budget and behind schedule"],3,"Cost variance is EV minus AC (320,000 - 400,000 = -80,000), so it is over budget. Schedule variance is EV minus PV (320,000 - 400,000 = -80,000), so it is behind schedule. Both CPI and SPI are 0.8.","Outline 3A1",["Both variances are negative, so the project is neither under budget nor ahead of schedule.","Actual cost exceeds earned value, so the project is over budget, not on budget.","Earned value is below planned value, so the project is behind schedule, not ahead.",null],null],["ca34",0,3,"At a mid-project review, costs have doubled and the expected benefits have fallen. What should the IS auditor recommend?",["Continue the project as planned because a lot of money has already been spent","Revisit the business case to confirm the project is still justified","Cut the testing phase to recover the schedule","Replace the project manager immediately"],1,"The business case should be revalidated at key milestones. Money already spent is a sunk cost; what matters is whether remaining investment still delivers enough value. Cutting testing raises risk.","Outline 3A2",["Continuing because of past spending is the sunk-cost fallacy; the decision should look at future costs and benefits.",null,"Cutting testing to save time increases the chance of defects and control failures in production.","Replacing the manager may or may not help, and it does not answer whether the project is still worth doing."],null],["ca35",0,3,"Which input control would BEST prevent a user from entering a hire date of 31 February?",["A hash total","A validity (date) check","A record count","A sequence check"],1,"A validity check rejects values that are not valid for the field, such as impossible dates. Hash totals and record counts verify completeness of batches, and sequence checks find missing or duplicate numbers.","Outline 3A4",["A hash total sums a non-financial field to detect changes to a batch; it does not check individual dates.",null,"A record count confirms the number of records processed, not whether a date is valid.","A sequence check looks for gaps or duplicates in numbered items, not impossible dates."],null],["ca36",0,3,"Which testing is performed by business users to confirm that a new system meets their requirements before go-live?",["Unit testing","User acceptance testing","Regression testing","Integration testing"],1,"User acceptance testing (UAT) is done by users and signed off by the system owner to confirm requirements are met. Unit and integration testing are done by developers or testers on components and their connections.","Outline 3B1",["Unit testing is done by developers on individual modules, not by business users against requirements.",null,"Regression testing checks that changes did not break existing functions; it is not the users' acceptance.","Integration testing checks that components work together; it is typically done by the project team."],null],["ca37",0,3,"A bank is replacing its core system and cannot tolerate processing errors, but has budget and staff to spare. Which changeover approach is MOST appropriate?",["Direct (big bang) cutover","Pilot changeover in one branch only","Parallel changeover","Abrupt changeover over a weekend"],2,"Parallel changeover runs both systems at once and compares results, giving the lowest risk when errors are unacceptable and resources allow. Direct cutover is cheapest but has no fallback.","Outline 3B2",["Direct cutover leaves no fallback if the new system produces errors, which the bank cannot tolerate.","A pilot limits exposure to one site but still runs that site without comparison against the old system.",null,"An abrupt weekend changeover is another name for direct cutover and carries the same risk."],null],["ca38",0,3,"What is the MOST important control during data conversion to a new system?",["Reconciling record counts and control totals","Deleting the old data as soon as conversion starts","Letting developers correct converted data directly","Converting data only after go-live"],0,"Reconciliation using record counts, control totals and hash totals proves the conversion was complete and accurate, with exceptions resolved and signed off. The old data should be kept until conversion is verified.","Outline 3B3",[null,"Deleting the source early removes the ability to verify or roll back the conversion.","Developers editing production data directly bypasses controls and creates a segregation-of-duties problem.","Data must be converted and verified before go-live, or the new system starts with missing data."],null],["ca39",0,3,"When is the BEST time to perform a post-implementation review of a new system?",["The day before go-live, once final testing is complete","During the requirements phase","Only if users complain","Once benefits can be measured in production"],3,"A post-implementation review compares actual benefits, costs and control operation with the business case. That requires the system to have operated long enough to produce meaningful results, often several months.","Outline 3B4",["Before go-live there are no production results to compare with the business case.","The requirements phase is far too early; nothing has been implemented yet.","Reviews should be planned, not triggered only by complaints, because silent control gaps would be missed.",null],null],["ca40",0,3,"In an agile project, what should an IS auditor expect to see in place of a large upfront requirements document?",["No documentation of any kind","A prioritized product backlog with acceptance criteria","A signed contract for every sprint","A full design frozen before development starts"],1,"Agile uses a product backlog of user stories, each with acceptance criteria, refined and prioritized throughout the project. It still produces documentation, just in smaller, evolving pieces.","Outline 3A3",["Agile favors working software but still needs documentation such as user stories and acceptance criteria.",null,"Separate signed contracts per sprint are not an agile practice.","Freezing the full design up front is a waterfall characteristic, not agile."],null],["ca41",0,3,"An IS auditor is asked to join a system development project. Which role preserves the auditor's independence?",["Advising on needed controls without designing them","Writing the application's access control code","Approving the system for production on behalf of users","Serving as project manager for the control workstream"],0,"Auditors can advise on needed controls and audit trails during development, but design, build and approval belong to management and users. Taking those roles would mean later auditing their own work.","Outline 3A4",[null,"Writing control code makes the auditor responsible for the control, which they would later have to audit.","Production approval belongs to the system owner and users; the auditor should not accept the system for them.","Managing part of the project is a management role that impairs independence."],null],["ca42",0,3,"After a code change to a payroll module, testers rerun earlier test cases to confirm nothing else broke. What is this called?",["Stress testing","Pilot testing","White-box testing","Regression testing"],3,"Regression testing reruns previous tests after a change to confirm existing functions still work. Stress testing checks behavior under heavy load, and white-box testing examines internal logic.","Outline 3B1",["Stress testing measures performance under extreme load, not whether old functions still work.","Pilot testing deploys to a limited group of users; it is not rerunning earlier test cases.","White-box testing looks at internal code paths; it is a technique, not the rerun of prior tests after a change.",null],null],["ca43",0,3,"Which is the GREATEST risk when a vendor's software is customized heavily before implementation?",["The software license becomes free","Users will need no training","The vendor will lose ownership rights to its own source code","Future vendor upgrades may be difficult and costly to apply"],3,"Heavy customization makes each vendor upgrade or patch harder to apply, because changes must be re-done and retested. This can leave the organization on unsupported, unpatched versions.","Outline 3A3",["Customization does not change license costs; if anything it adds cost.","Customized software still requires user training, often more than standard software.","The vendor keeps its source code; customization does not remove it.",null],null],["ca44",0,4,"While testing change management, an auditor compares the production system's change log with approved change tickets. Several changes have no ticket. What does this MOST likely indicate?",["Efficient use of the change process","A problem with the backup schedule","Unauthorized changes to production","Normal emergency change handling"],2,"Changes in production with no matching approved ticket are unauthorized until shown otherwise. Even emergency changes should be documented and approved after the fact.","Outline 4A8",["Changes that bypass the change process are a control failure, not efficiency.","Backup scheduling is unrelated to whether production changes were authorized.",null,"Emergency changes still need a ticket and retroactive approval, so missing tickets are not normal."],null],["ca45",0,4,"Which is the PRIMARY goal of problem management?",["Finding and removing the root cause of recurring incidents","Restoring normal service to affected users as quickly as possible","Logging every user request for help","Approving changes before implementation"],0,"Problem management investigates underlying causes so incidents stop recurring. Restoring service quickly is the goal of incident management, which may use workarounds without fixing the cause.","Outline 4A7",[null,"Restoring service quickly is incident management's goal; problem management looks for the underlying cause.","Logging requests is a service desk function, not the purpose of problem management.","Approving changes is the job of change management."],null],["ca46",0,4,"An organization can lose no more than 15 minutes of order data. Which backup or recovery approach BEST meets this?",["Weekly full backups stored offsite in a secure facility","Monthly tape backups kept in a vault","Near-continuous replication to a second site","Daily incremental backups at midnight"],2,"A 15-minute recovery point objective (RPO) means data must be copied at least every 15 minutes, which requires replication or frequent transaction log backups. Daily or weekly backups could lose up to a day or a week of data.","Outline 4B3",["Weekly full backups could lose up to a week of orders, far beyond a 15-minute RPO.","Monthly backups would risk losing up to a month of data.",null,"Daily incrementals could lose nearly 24 hours of orders."],null],["ca47",0,4,"What should be done FIRST when developing a business continuity plan?",["Sign a contract for a hot site","Perform a business impact analysis","Buy additional backup hardware","Run a full interruption test"],1,"The business impact analysis (BIA) identifies critical processes, their impact over time and their RTO and RPO. Recovery strategies such as hot sites and hardware purchases follow from it, and testing comes after the plan is written.","Outline 4B1",["A recovery site should be chosen based on RTOs set by the BIA; signing first may over- or under-spend.",null,"Hardware purchases should support a strategy derived from the BIA, not precede it.","A full interruption test is the most disruptive test and happens only after a plan exists and simpler tests pass."],null],["ca48",0,4,"Which recovery site can resume operations within hours because it has equipment, data and connectivity ready?",["Cold site","Hot site","Warm site with no data","Reciprocal agreement"],1,"A hot site is fully equipped and has current or near-current data, so it can take over within hours. A cold site has only space and power, and reciprocal agreements are rarely reliable or tested.","Outline 4B5",["A cold site has space, power and cooling but no equipment or data, so recovery takes weeks.",null,"A warm site has some equipment but needs data restored and configuration, taking days rather than hours.","Reciprocal agreements depend on another organization's spare capacity and are hard to enforce or test."],null],["ca49",0,4,"The finance team uses a complex spreadsheet to calculate quarterly revenue figures for the board. What is the auditor's PRIMARY concern?",["No change control, testing or access control over it","The cost of the spreadsheet software license","The spreadsheet's file format being outdated and poorly supported","The number of worksheets in the file"],0,"End-user computing tools often support key decisions but lack the controls of formal systems: versioning, testing, access restriction and backup. Errors can go undetected and flow into board reporting.","Outline 4A5",[null,"License cost is minor compared with the risk of undetected errors in board-level figures.","File format is a technical detail that does not address the integrity of the calculations.","The number of worksheets is not a control concern by itself."],null],["ca50",0,4,"An IT service level agreement promises 99.9% availability each month. What evidence BEST shows whether the SLA is being met?",["Independent monitoring data on actual uptime compared with the target","The service provider's marketing brochure describing its data centers","A verbal assurance from the service manager","The number of staff on the support team"],0,"Measured availability from reliable monitoring, compared against the SLA target, shows whether the commitment is being met. Promises and staffing levels do not measure actual performance.","Outline 4A10",[null,"Marketing material describes intent, not measured performance.","Verbal assurances are weak evidence and need corroboration with data.","Staffing levels may affect service but do not show actual availability."],null],["ca51",0,4,"Which is the MOST effective way to verify that backups can actually be used for recovery?",["Check that backup jobs report success","Periodically restore data from backup and verify it","Keep backup media in a fireproof safe","Increase the backup retention period"],1,"Only a test restore proves that backup data is complete, readable and usable within the recovery time needed. A job reporting success does not prove the data can be restored.","Outline 4B3",["A success status shows the job ran, not that the data is complete or restorable.",null,"Protecting media from fire helps availability but does not prove the backups are usable.","Longer retention keeps more copies but says nothing about whether they can be restored."],null],["ca52",0,4,"A batch interface transfers daily sales from the point-of-sale system to the general ledger. Which control BEST ensures all records arrive?",["Encrypting the transfer file with a strong algorithm before sending","Running the interface at night","Reconciling record counts and control totals at both ends","Giving the interface a service account"],2,"Record counts and control totals compared at both ends prove completeness and accuracy of the transfer. Encryption protects confidentiality in transit but does not prove all records were received.","Outline 4A4",["Encryption protects confidentiality, but a file can be encrypted and still be incomplete.","Scheduling at night reduces load but does nothing to confirm completeness.",null,"A service account is about access, not about whether all records were transferred."],null],["ca53",0,4,"Which person should NOT have update access to the production job scheduler?",["An application developer","The production operations lead","The scheduling administrator","A batch operator on shift"],0,"Developers should not be able to change production schedules or run jobs in production, which would let them bypass change control. Operations staff maintain the schedule as part of their duties.","Outline 4A3",[null,"The operations lead is expected to manage production scheduling.","The scheduling administrator's job is to maintain the scheduler.","Batch operators run and monitor scheduled jobs as part of their normal duties."],null],["ca54",0,4,"An auditor finds that software is installed on 250 workstations but only 180 licenses were purchased. What is the PRIMARY risk?",["Slower workstation performance","Legal and financial exposure from license noncompliance","Higher electricity costs","Reduced network bandwidth"],1,"Using more copies than licensed exposes the organization to vendor audits, penalties and reputational harm. This is why software asset management tracks installations against entitlements.","Outline 4A2",["Unlicensed copies do not inherently slow workstations.",null,"Electricity costs are unrelated to licensing.","Installed software does not by itself reduce network bandwidth."],null],["ca55",0,4,"A database table stores the customer's address in every order row, causing inconsistent addresses. Which technique addresses this?",["Normalization","Denormalization","Adding more indexes","Increasing the table size"],0,"Normalization organizes data so each fact is stored once, reducing redundancy and update anomalies such as inconsistent addresses. Denormalization deliberately adds redundancy for performance.","Outline 4A11",[null,"Denormalization adds redundancy, which would make the inconsistency worse.","Indexes speed up searches but do not remove duplicated data.","Table size has nothing to do with redundant data."],null],["ca56",0,4,"Which BCP test has participants discuss their roles around a table in response to a scenario, without any systems being affected?",["Full interruption test","Parallel test","Tabletop exercise","Recovery site cutover"],2,"A tabletop (walk-through) exercise has team members talk through a scenario to check their understanding of the plan. It is low cost and does not disrupt operations, unlike parallel or full interruption tests.","Outline 4B4",["A full interruption test actually shuts down primary operations, which is the most disruptive test.","A parallel test brings up recovery systems and processes data alongside production.",null,"A cutover to the recovery site moves real processing and is not a discussion exercise."],null],["ca57",0,4,"A critical patch is released for an internet-facing server. What should happen BEFORE it is deployed to production?",["Deploy it immediately without any record","Wait for the next scheduled annual maintenance window to avoid any outage","Test it in a non-production environment and follow change management","Ask users whether they want the patch"],2,"Patches should be tested for compatibility and deployed through change management, using an expedited or emergency path when risk is high. Skipping records bypasses control, and waiting a year leaves a known vulnerability open.","Outline 4A8",["Deploying without testing or records risks outages and undocumented changes.","Waiting for an annual window leaves a critical, known vulnerability exposed for too long.",null,"Patch decisions are made through risk and change processes, not by polling users."],null],["ca58",0,4,"System logs on a critical server are stored locally and overwritten every three days. What is the MAIN audit concern?",["Logs use too much storage","Log timestamps may be too precise","Evidence needed for investigations may be lost","The server may run faster without logs"],2,"Short local retention means events may be overwritten before anyone reviews them or before an incident is discovered. Logs should be forwarded to a protected central store and kept per the retention policy.","Outline 4A9",["Storage use is a minor concern compared with losing evidence.","Precise timestamps help investigations; they are not a problem.",null,"Performance is not the audit concern; losing the audit trail is."],null],["ca59",0,4,"Which BEST describes the recovery time objective (RTO)?",["The maximum acceptable amount of data loss measured in time since the last backup","The average time between system failures","The maximum acceptable time to restore a system after a disruption","The time needed to back up all data"],2,"RTO is how quickly a system must be restored. RPO is the maximum data loss measured in time, and mean time between failures is a reliability measure.","Outline 4B1",["The maximum acceptable data loss measured in time is the RPO.","Average time between failures is MTBF, a reliability measure.",null,"Backup duration is an operational metric, not a recovery objective."],null],["ca60",0,4,"A database administrator can modify data directly in production tables without leaving a trace. What is the BEST compensating control?",["Asking the DBA to sign a confidentiality and acceptable use agreement","Database activity logging reviewed by someone independent of the DBA","Giving the DBA a stronger password","Moving the database to a faster server"],1,"When a privileged role must keep powerful access, an independent review of logged DBA activity detects misuse. The logs must be protected so the DBA cannot alter them.","Outline 4A11",["A confidentiality agreement is a deterrent and does not detect unauthorized changes.",null,"A stronger password protects the account from others but does not control what the DBA does.","Server performance has no bearing on detecting unauthorized data changes."],null],["ca61",0,4,"Which approach BEST reduces the impact of a single disk failure on a database server?",["RAID 0 striping across disks","RAID with redundancy such as mirroring or parity","Compressing the database files","Scheduling more frequent reboots"],1,"RAID levels with redundancy (for example RAID 1, 5, 6 or 10) keep data available when a disk fails. RAID 0 stripes without redundancy, so one failed disk loses the whole array.","Outline 4B2",["RAID 0 has no redundancy, so a single disk failure destroys the whole array.",null,"Compression saves space but gives no protection against disk failure.","Rebooting does not protect data against a failed disk."],null],["ca62",0,4,"Capacity reports show storage use on a core server growing 8% per month with 25% free space left. What should the auditor expect management to do?",["Wait until the disk is completely full, then react to the outage","Plan capacity upgrades based on the trend before space runs out","Delete audit logs to free space","Stop monitoring capacity to save effort"],1,"Capacity management uses trends to forecast needs and act before resources are exhausted. At 8% monthly growth, space runs out in roughly three months, so planning should start now. Deleting audit logs destroys evidence.","Outline 4A6",["Reacting after the disk fills causes an outage that capacity management exists to prevent.",null,"Deleting audit logs destroys evidence and may violate retention requirements.","Stopping monitoring removes the information needed to manage capacity."],null],["ca63",0,4,"Business units are subscribing to cloud applications with corporate credit cards without IT's knowledge. What is the BEST first step?",["Block all internet access for those units","Ignore it because cloud providers are responsible for all security","Discover the unapproved services and assess their risk","Fire the managers who approved the purchases"],2,"Shadow IT must first be found and assessed so that risky services can be brought under governance or replaced. Blanket blocking harms the business, and cloud providers do not take over the customer's responsibilities.","Outline 4A5",["Blocking all internet access would stop legitimate work and is disproportionate as a first step.","Under the shared responsibility model the customer still owns its data, access and configuration.",null,"Disciplinary action is a management matter and does not address the risk from the services in use."],null],["ca64",0,4,"What is the MOST important reason to keep an accurate IT asset inventory?",["It lowers the price of new hardware","It removes the need for patching","It replaces the configuration management database","You cannot protect or manage assets you do not know you have"],3,"An accurate inventory is the foundation for patching, licensing, security monitoring and lifecycle management. Unknown assets go unpatched and unmonitored.","Outline 4A2",["An inventory does not change vendor prices.","Knowing assets exist is what makes patching possible; it does not remove the need.","An asset inventory often feeds the CMDB; it does not replace it.",null],null],["ca65",0,4,"After a major disaster recovery test, several recovery steps failed. What should happen NEXT?",["Declare the test a success because some systems recovered","Cancel future tests to avoid disruption","Keep the results confidential from management","Update the plan to fix the gaps and retest"],3,"Tests exist to find weaknesses. Results should be reported to management, the plan corrected, and the affected parts retested. Hiding or ignoring failures defeats the purpose of testing.","Outline 4B5",["Calling a partial failure a success hides real gaps that would matter in an actual disaster.","Stopping tests leaves the organization unsure whether it can recover at all.","Management needs test results to make decisions about recovery risk.",null],null],["ca66",0,4,"An organization keeps backup copies only in the same building as its data center. What is the MAIN risk?",["Backups will take longer to run","Backup software licenses will cost more for on-premises storage","Restores will be too fast to verify","A site-wide disaster could destroy both the data and its backups"],3,"Backups need to be stored offsite or in another region so that a fire, flood or ransomware incident affecting one site does not destroy both production and backup copies.","Outline 4B3",["Backup duration is not affected by where copies are stored after the job runs.","Storage location does not change license costs.","Fast restores are a benefit, not a risk.",null],null],["ca67",0,4,"Which change management practice BEST prevents a developer from moving untested code into production?",["Letting developers approve their own changes","Using the same account in test and production","Allowing direct edits in production for speed","Separating the migration role from the development role"],3,"When a separate person or automated pipeline with approval gates moves code to production, untested or unapproved code cannot be deployed by the developer alone. The other options remove that separation.","Outline 4A8",["Self-approval defeats the purpose of change approval.","Shared accounts across environments blur accountability and let changes flow unchecked.","Direct edits in production bypass testing and change control entirely.",null],null],["ca68",0,5,"During an access review, the auditor finds 14 active accounts belonging to people who left the company months ago. What is the BEST recommendation?",["Force all users to change passwords every 30 days","Run an access review once every three years","Require longer passwords for all accounts","Tie HR terminations to automatic account removal"],3,"The root cause is a broken leaver process. Tying HR termination events to account removal, backed by periodic reviews, fixes it. Password changes do nothing about accounts that should not exist.","Outline 5A3",["Password rotation does not remove accounts of people who have left.","Reviewing less often would let orphaned accounts persist even longer.","Password length does not address accounts that should have been disabled.",null],null],["ca69",0,5,"Who should perform the periodic review of user access rights to a financial application?",["The user whose access is being reviewed","The help desk team that creates accounts","The external auditor","The data or application owner"],3,"The owner knows which access each role needs and is accountable for the data, so they certify that access is still appropriate. Account administrators implement changes but should not certify their own work.","Outline 5A3",["Users reviewing their own access is self-approval and provides no independent check.","The team that creates accounts would be reviewing its own work, which weakens the control.","External auditors test the review process; performing it would make them part of the control.",null],null],["ca70",0,5,"Which provides the STRONGEST authentication for remote administrator access?",["A long password changed monthly","Two different passwords","A password plus a security question","A password plus a hardware security key"],3,"Multifactor authentication combines different factor types. A password (something you know) plus a hardware key (something you have) is far stronger than any combination of knowledge factors, which can all be phished or guessed.","Outline 5A3",["A single password is one factor, however long or often changed.","Two passwords are two instances of the same factor type, so this is not true multifactor.","A security question is another knowledge factor and is often guessable from public information.",null],null],["ca71",0,5,"An auditor reviewing a firewall rule base finds a rule near the top that allows any source to any destination on any port. What is the MAIN concern?",["It slows down the firewall's logging","It defeats the deny-by-default policy for all later rules","It prevents administrators from logging in to manage the firewall","It makes the rule base easier to read"],1,"Firewalls usually process rules top-down, so an any-any allow rule near the top permits all traffic and makes later restrictive rules meaningless. Rule sets should end in an explicit deny and have owners for each rule.","Outline 5A4",["Logging performance is minor compared with allowing all traffic through.",null,"An allow-all rule does not block administrative logins; it permits too much.","Readability is not the concern; the rule opens the network completely."],null],["ca72",0,5,"Which cryptographic method provides non-repudiation for an electronic purchase order?",["Symmetric encryption with a shared key","A digital signature using the sender's private key","A hash of the order without a key","Encrypting the order with the receiver's public key"],1,"Only the sender holds their private key, so a valid signature proves who signed and that the content was not altered. A shared symmetric key could have been used by either party, so it cannot prove which one sent the message.","Outline 5A7",["With a shared key, either party could have created the message, so it cannot prove who sent it.",null,"A plain hash shows integrity but anyone can compute it, so it does not identify the sender.","Encrypting with the receiver's public key gives confidentiality, not proof of who sent the order."],null],["ca73",0,5,"What is the purpose of a certificate revocation list (CRL)?",["To flag certificates that are no longer trusted before expiry","To list every certificate the certificate authority has ever issued","To store users' private keys for recovery","To record which websites use TLS"],0,"A CRL, or its online equivalent OCSP, lets relying parties check whether a certificate has been revoked, for example because its private key was compromised. Private keys should never be stored by the CA for this purpose.","Outline 5A7",[null,"A CRL lists only revoked certificates, not every certificate issued.","Key escrow or recovery is a separate function; CRLs never hold private keys.","CRLs are about revoked certificates, not about which sites use TLS."],null],["ca74",0,5,"Which control BEST protects data on laptops that could be lost or stolen?",["Full-disk encryption","A screen saver password","Antivirus software","A cable lock at the office"],0,"Full-disk encryption keeps data unreadable without the key even if the drive is removed. A screen saver password is bypassed by removing the disk, and antivirus does not protect against physical theft.","Outline 5A6",[null,"A screen lock does not protect data if the thief removes the drive and reads it elsewhere.","Antivirus protects against malware, not against someone reading a stolen disk.","A cable lock only helps while the laptop is at the desk, not when it is lost while traveling."],null],["ca75",0,5,"A data loss prevention (DLP) tool is deployed in monitoring-only mode and generates thousands of alerts no one reviews. What is the auditor's MAIN concern?",["The tool's license is too expensive","The alerts use too much disk space","Users may find the tool annoying","The control provides little real protection"],3,"A detective control only works if its alerts are tuned and acted on. Untriaged alerts mean data leaks go unnoticed, so the control exists on paper but not in practice.","Outline 5A5",["Cost is secondary to the fact that the control is not working.","Storage use is a minor operational matter compared with unnoticed data leaks.","In monitoring mode users are not even interrupted; the issue is that nobody acts on alerts.",null],null],["ca76",0,5,"Which physical control BEST prevents an unauthorized person from following an employee into the data center?",["A visitor sign-in sheet","A mantrap (access control vestibule)","A security camera at the door","A sign saying authorized staff only"],1,"A mantrap lets only one person pass at a time after authentication, which stops tailgating. Cameras and sign-in sheets can help detect or deter but do not physically prevent entry.","Outline 5A2",["A sign-in sheet records visitors but does not stop someone slipping in behind an employee.",null,"A camera records tailgating after the fact; it does not physically prevent it.","A sign is a deterrent only and physically stops no one."],null],["ca77",0,5,"Which fire suppression approach is generally preferred for a staffed data center because it limits water damage while being safe for people?",["A wet-pipe sprinkler system","Carbon dioxide flooding","Portable extinguishers only","A pre-action sprinkler system"],3,"A pre-action system keeps pipes dry until a detector triggers and a sprinkler head opens, reducing the risk of accidental water discharge. Carbon dioxide flooding is dangerous to people in the room.","Outline 5A2",["Wet-pipe systems hold water in the pipes, so a damaged head or leak can soak equipment.","Carbon dioxide displaces oxygen and is hazardous in occupied spaces.","Portable extinguishers alone are not an adequate suppression system for a data center.",null],null],["ca78",0,5,"An organization moves its HR system to a SaaS provider. Which responsibility stays with the organization?",["Managing which users have access to the application","Patching the provider's hypervisors","Securing the provider's physical data center and its perimeter","Maintaining the provider's network hardware"],0,"In SaaS, the provider runs the infrastructure and application, but the customer still controls its users, their access rights and its data. Accountability for the data never moves to the provider.","Outline 5A8",[null,"Hypervisor patching is the provider's job in SaaS.","Physical data center security is always the provider's responsibility in public cloud.","Network hardware in the provider's facility is managed by the provider."],null],["ca79",0,5,"Employees receive an email that appears to come from the CEO urgently requesting a wire transfer to a new account. What type of attack is this?",["Denial of service","SQL injection","Business email compromise","Cross-site scripting"],2,"Business email compromise uses spoofed or compromised executive or vendor email to trick staff into sending money or data. Defenses include verification call-backs on a known number and dual approval of payments.","Outline 5B2",["Denial of service floods a system to make it unavailable; it does not request payments.","SQL injection attacks database queries in applications, not people through email.",null,"Cross-site scripting injects scripts into web pages; it is not a payment fraud email."],null],["ca80",0,5,"What is the BEST measure of a security awareness program's effectiveness?",["The number of posters displayed","Attendance rates at the mandatory annual training session","Trends in phishing simulation click and report rates","The length of the training video"],2,"Effectiveness is shown by behavior change, such as fewer clicks and more reports in phishing simulations over time. Attendance and posters measure activity, not whether people behave more securely.","Outline 5B1",["Posters show effort, not whether behavior changed.","Attendance proves people were present, not that they learned or changed behavior.",null,"Video length says nothing about outcomes."],null],["ca81",0,5,"Before an external penetration test begins, what is MOST important to have in place?",["Written authorization and agreed rules of engagement","A list of every employee's password","The tester's personal social media accounts","Disabled logging so the test activity is not recorded anywhere"],0,"Without written authorization and a defined scope, a penetration test may be illegal and could disrupt production. Rules of engagement set scope, timing, contacts and data handling.","Outline 5B3",[null,"Testers should never be handed user passwords; that is not a prerequisite and creates new risk.","Personal social media accounts are irrelevant to authorizing a test.","Logging should stay on so the test can be told apart from real attacks and reviewed afterward."],null],["ca82",0,5,"Which technique BEST helps correlate security events from firewalls, servers and applications to detect an attack in progress?",["A security information and event management (SIEM) system","Manual review of each log once a year","Disabling logs on low-risk systems","Storing all logs on the systems that create them"],0,"A SIEM collects logs centrally, normalizes them and applies correlation rules to raise alerts on suspicious patterns across sources. Local-only logs are hard to correlate and easy for an attacker to erase.","Outline 5B4",[null,"An annual manual review is far too slow to catch an attack in progress.","Disabling logs removes visibility attackers can exploit, including on low-risk systems used as stepping stones.","Local storage prevents correlation across sources and lets attackers delete evidence."],null],["ca83",0,5,"During a malware incident, the response team wants to preserve evidence from a running server. Which data should be captured FIRST?",["Archived backup tapes","The printed system manual","Last year's audit report","The contents of memory"],3,"Evidence should be collected in order of volatility, from most to least volatile. Memory contents are lost when the system is powered off, so they come before disk images and archives.","Outline 5B6",["Backup tapes are stable and can be collected later.","A printed manual is not evidence of the incident.","An old audit report is background information, not volatile evidence.",null],null],["ca84",0,5,"Why is chain of custody documentation important for digital evidence?",["It shows the evidence was handled properly and not altered","It speeds up the recovery of systems","It encrypts the evidence automatically","It replaces the need to calculate a hash value of the evidence"],0,"Chain of custody records who collected, transferred and stored evidence and when, which supports its integrity and admissibility. Hashing complements it by proving the data has not changed.","Outline 5B6",[null,"Chain of custody supports legal use of evidence; it does not speed up recovery.","Documentation does not encrypt anything.","Hashing and custody records work together; neither replaces the other."],null],["ca85",0,5,"An auditor finds that IoT sensors on the factory floor share the same flat network as the finance servers. What is the BEST recommendation?",["Replace all sensors with laptops","Segment the IoT devices into their own network zone","Turn off the finance servers at night","Give each sensor an administrator account"],1,"IoT devices are often hard to patch and weakly secured, so isolating them in their own segment with filtered traffic limits what an attacker can reach if one is compromised.","Outline 5A9",["Replacing sensors with laptops is impractical and does not address network exposure.",null,"Powering off servers at night harms availability and leaves them exposed during the day.","Administrator accounts on sensors increase risk rather than reduce it."],null],["ca86",0,5,"Which is the PRIMARY difference between a vulnerability scan and a penetration test?",["A penetration test tries to exploit weaknesses to show real impact","A vulnerability scan always requires physical access to each target system","A penetration test never needs authorization","A vulnerability scan fixes the weaknesses it finds"],0,"A vulnerability scan identifies and lists potential weaknesses, usually automatically. A penetration test goes further by attempting controlled exploitation to demonstrate what an attacker could actually achieve, and it always needs authorization.","Outline 5B3",[null,"Vulnerability scans are usually run over the network and do not require physical access.","Penetration tests always require written authorization.","Scanners report weaknesses; they do not fix them."],null],["ca87",0,5,"An incident response team has contained a ransomware outbreak. What should they do BEFORE restoring systems from backup?",["Confirm backups are clean and attacker access is removed","Pay the ransom to get the decryption key as quickly as possible","Delete all logs to save space","Announce the incident on social media"],0,"Restoring from infected backups or while the attacker still has access leads to reinfection. Eradication and verification of clean backups come before recovery.","Outline 5B5",[null,"Paying the ransom is a management and legal decision, does not guarantee recovery and is not a prerequisite for restoring from backup.","Logs are evidence needed for investigation and lessons learned.","Public communication follows the communication plan and legal advice, not an ad hoc post."],null],["ca88",0,5,"Which access control principle is violated when all employees in finance can view the entire payroll file?",["Defense in depth","Least privilege","Separation of environments","Fail secure"],1,"Least privilege gives users only the access needed for their job. Most finance staff do not need to see everyone's pay, so broad read access violates it.","Outline 5A3",["Defense in depth is about layering controls, not how much access each user gets.",null,"Separation of environments keeps development, test and production apart; it is not about user access scope.","Fail secure describes how a system behaves when it fails, not how access is granted."],null],["ca89",0,5,"Which feature of a mobile device management (MDM) solution BEST protects company data if an employee's phone is stolen?",["A custom company wallpaper","Automatic app store updates","Showing the device's battery level","Remote wipe of the corporate container or device"],3,"Remote wipe removes corporate data from a lost or stolen device, especially when combined with enforced screen locks and encryption. The other features do nothing to protect data after theft.","Outline 5A9",["A wallpaper is cosmetic and provides no protection.","App updates help patching but do not protect data on a stolen phone.","Battery level reporting has no security value after theft.",null],null],["ca90",0,5,"Which is the BEST reason to use a security baseline (hardening standard) for servers?",["It removes the need for vulnerability scanning","It guarantees servers cannot be attacked","It ensures servers are configured consistently to a known secure state","It lets each administrator choose their own preferred settings for each server"],2,"A baseline defines minimum secure settings so every server starts from the same hardened state, and configuration reviews can compare actual settings to it. It reduces but never removes risk.","Outline 5A1",["Baselines reduce weaknesses but new vulnerabilities still appear, so scanning is still needed.","No configuration guarantees immunity from attack.",null,"Letting each administrator choose settings is exactly the inconsistency a baseline prevents."],null]]);
